## 9. Quick Comparison Table

| Strategy | Complexity | Stale Data Risk | Best For |
|----------|-----------|-----------------|----------|
| **TTL Only** | ⭐ Very Low | High (TTL window) | Static data, beginners |
| **TTL + Explicit Delete** | ⭐⭐ Low | Very Low | Most production apps |
| **Pub/Sub Events** | ⭐⭐⭐ Medium | Low | Microservices, multi-server |
| **Keyspace Notifications** | ⭐⭐ Low-Medium | Low | Monitoring, automation |
| **Redis Streams** | ⭐⭐⭐⭐ High | Very Low | Critical, scalable systems |
| **Tag-Based** | ⭐⭐⭐ Medium | Low | Complex object relationships |

---

## 10. My Recommendation for a Beginner

### Start here:
1. **Use TTL + Explicit Invalidation** (Strategy #2)
   - Set a reasonable TTL (e.g., 5-10 minutes) as a safety net
   - Delete cache keys immediately when you update the database
   - This covers 90% of real-world use cases

### Then level up:
2. If you have **multiple servers/services**, add **Pub/Sub** (Strategy #3) so all instances get notified
3. If you need **guaranteed delivery**, switch to **Redis Streams**
4. If you have **complex relationships** (e.g., a user has posts, comments, likes), explore **Tag-Based** invalidation

---

## 11. Common Beginner Mistakes to Avoid

| Mistake | Why It's Bad | Fix |
|---------|-------------|-----|
| **Updating DB but forgetting cache** | Users see old data forever | Always invalidate after DB write |
| **Deleting cache BEFORE updating DB** | If DB write fails, cache is gone | Update DB first, then delete cache |
| **No TTL at all** | Cache grows forever, stale data persists | Always set a TTL as backup |
| **Using TTL alone for critical data** | Users see wrong prices, balances, etc. | Add explicit invalidation |
| **Invalidating the whole cache** (`FLUSHDB`) | Massive DB load | Invalidate only changed keys |

